<h2>16. Best Practices</h2>
<ul>
<li>Be explicit in robots.txt. Name the bots you care about and place their rules before wildcard rules.</li>
<li>Server-render your answers. Critical content should be in the initial HTML, not injected by JavaScript.</li>
<li>Write for extraction. One idea per paragraph, descriptive headings, lists, tables, and concrete specifics.</li>
<li>Show your work. Author bylines, dates, outbound citations, and schema markup all increase trust.</li>
<li>Keep content fresh. Update high-traffic pages regularly and surface the last-updated date.</li>
<li>Use semantic HTML. &lt;main&gt;, &lt;article&gt;, and logical heading hierarchy help extractors find the real content.</li>
<li>Provide a clean llms.txt. Point AI systems to your best, most authoritative content in markdown form.</li>
<li>Test continuously. Fetch as AI crawlers, score citation readiness, and iterate.</li>
<li>Fix access before content. A blocked page cannot be cited, no matter how well it is written.</li>
<li>Think in passages, not pages. The unit of competition is the extractable answer, not the entire article.</li>
</ul>

<h2>17. FAQs</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>What is an AI crawler?</strong><br>
An AI crawler is a bot that fetches web pages and extracts text for large language models. It reads raw HTML, strips decoration, and converts content into chunks models can retrieve. Examples include GPTBot, OAI-SearchBot, ClaudeBot, and PerplexityBot. Unlike human browsers, AI crawlers care about structure and text, not visuals.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>How is an AI crawler different from Googlebot?</strong><br>
Googlebot renders JavaScript deeply and ranks whole pages using hundreds of signals. AI crawlers usually fetch static HTML and focus on extracting citable passages. Their success is measured by citation frequency, not search position. The unit of value is the passage, not the page.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Do AI crawlers execute JavaScript?</strong><br>
Most AI crawlers do not execute JavaScript, or only in a very limited way. If your content renders client-side, crawlers may see an empty or partial page. Server-side rendering puts the answer text into the initial HTML. That is the safest way to ensure AI crawlers can read your content.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Will blocking GPTBot prevent me from being cited by ChatGPT?</strong><br>
No, blocking GPTBot does not stop ChatGPT from citing your page. GPTBot is used to train future models, not to power live search citations. ChatGPT search uses OAI-SearchBot and ChatGPT-User for citations. You can block GPTBot while allowing the search and browsing bots.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>What is llms.txt and do I need one?</strong><br>
llms.txt is a markdown file at your domain root that points AI systems to your best content. It acts like a curated directory for language models, not a replacement for crawlable pages. It is optional but useful for signaling intent and improving discovery. You still need readable, high-quality content on your actual pages.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>How do I know if an AI crawler can read my site?</strong><br>
Fetch your pages using the exact user-agent of each AI crawler you care about. Compare the returned text to what a normal browser sees. If the crawler preview is thin, missing, or different, you have a rendering or access problem. Tools like Ollagraph's LLM fetch simulator automate this check.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Does schema markup help with AI citations?</strong><br>
Schema markup reduces ambiguity and strengthens trust signals for AI models. Article schema with author, datePublished, dateModified, and publisher is especially useful. It is not a direct ranking trigger, but it supports the signals models already prefer. Well-structured metadata makes your page easier to understand and cite.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>How often should I update content for AI crawlers?</strong><br>
Update high-traffic and time-sensitive pages whenever facts, products, or guidance change. Add a visible last-updated date and matching dateModified schema. Fresh content wins for recent queries because models prefer current sources. Stale pages are not wrong, but they are less likely to be cited.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Can I pay to be cited by AI answer engines?</strong><br>
No, there is no paid placement system for AI citations. Citations are earned through crawl access, content quality, specificity, and trust. You cannot buy a citation from ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Claude. The only path is to make your pages reachable, readable, and worth citing.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>What is the fastest way to improve AI crawler visibility?</strong><br>
Start with an allowlist audit to confirm crawlers can reach your pages. Then run a fetch simulator to check whether they can actually read your content. Finally, score citation readiness and fix the highest-impact content issues. Work in that exact order: access first, readability second, content quality third.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Should I create separate pages for AI crawlers?</strong><br>
No, you should never create separate pages or serve different content just for AI crawlers. That is cloaking, and it damages trust with both crawlers and search engines. Optimize your real pages so they work for humans and machines together. The same content should serve both audiences cleanly.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>How does Ollagraph help with AI crawler preparation?</strong><br>
Ollagraph provides APIs and tools to audit AI bot access, simulate crawler fetches, and score citation readiness. It also generates llms.txt files and converts pages into LLM-ready markdown. These tools turn assumptions into measurable fixes. You can test every gate instead of guessing.</p>
</li>
</ol>

<h2>18. Conclusion</h2>
<p>AI crawlers read websites by stripping away everything that makes a page pretty and keeping only what makes it informative. They do not care about your hero animation, your color palette, or your font choice. They care whether the answer is in the HTML, whether it is specific, and whether they are allowed to take it.</p>
<p>Preparing content for LLMs is therefore a discipline of subtraction and structure. Subtract the barriers that keep crawlers out. Subtract the JavaScript that hides your answers. Subtract the vague prose that models cannot quote. Then structure what remains with clear headings, short paragraphs, lists, tables, and honest metadata.</p>
<p>The reward is not a better position on a search results page. It is inclusion inside the answers that millions of users now read first. That is a new channel, and it is winnable by the teams that treat AI crawler readiness as engineering, not marketing.</p>
<p>Start with access. Confirm readability. Then make every page worth citing. The tools to test each step already exist. The only mistake is assuming you do not need them.</p>

